UPDATE artists SET bio_en = 'Francisco Saldaña — known as Luny, born on 23 June 1979 in San Pedro de Macorís — is a Dominican record producer, one half of Luny Tunes, and, in the decade after the duo’s classic period, a hit producer in his own right.

**A childhood on the move**

His stepfather was a dangerous, jealous man who kept coming after Saldaña’s mother, and the family moved more than ten times to get away from him. His mother never stopped working to keep the family afloat, and Saldaña has credited that example for his own drive. One of those moves took the family through Puerto Rico, where he spent part of his adolescence in a barrio of San Juan immersed in the city’s underground scene — the same scene reggaeton itself came out of — before they settled in Massachusetts.

**Boston, then Puerto Rico**

In Massachusetts a mutual friend introduced him to Víctor Cabrera, and the two started working together right away. Both took jobs at a Harvard University dining hall, and it took them close to a year to leave — they were well liked by their co-workers, and it was not an easy decision. On a trip back to Puerto Rico, Saldaña ran into DJ Nelson in a studio and showed him some of his production; Nelson happened to be looking for someone to help him record vocals and build tracks, and Saldaña went home to Boston with the news. Their first session with Nelson was on 31 December 2001, and Nelson did not yet know who Cabrera was. New to San Juan and knowing no one, Cabrera preferred to stay home rather than come to the studio at first; working alongside Noriega, the two split the work like a musical yin and yang, Cabrera building the melodies and Saldaña driving the drums. Their first production credit came in 2002, on the compilation «Los Matadores del Género», with «Pasto» and «Pelea» for Don Omar and «Chica Ven» for Plan B — and the duo’s name reportedly settled as «Luny Tunes», rather than the reverse, only because Saldaña insisted on it.

**Piracy, threats, and a studio in the yard**

In 2003, unreleased tracks by major reggaeton artists began circulating on pirated discs, and Tego Calderón, Wisin y Yandel and Héctor «El Bambino» blamed Nelson and the duo for the leak, to the point of death threats. Saldaña and Cabrera fled to Boston, and guilt over leaving without an explanation pushed Saldaña to go back and apologize. Once things had calmed down they returned to Puerto Rico and built their own studio in the yard of Saldaña’s mother’s house, and it was there that they produced Tego Calderón’s «El Abayarde», Héctor & Tito’s «A la reconquista» and, with Eliel, Don Omar’s debut «The Last Don» — work that drew Universal Music Group’s attention and led to Saldaña and Cabrera’s own label, Mas Flow Inc., and the end of their arrangement with Nelson.

**A solo run**

As the duo’s classic era wound down, Saldaña kept producing under his own name: Daddy Yankee’s «Prestige» (2012) and «Legendaddy» (2022), Yandel’s «De líder a leyenda» (2013) and «Quién contra mí 2» (2020), Dyland & Lenny’s «My World 2» (2013), Tito el Bambino’s «El muñeco» (2020), Enrique Iglesias’s «Final (Vol. 1)» (2021), Sharlene’s self-titled album (2016), Natti Natasha’s «Nattividad» (2021) and Becky G’s «Esquemas» (2022) — a run the duo’s own discography lists as his alone, with nothing similar credited to Cabrera.

**Legacy**

What Saldaña built with Cabrera is told in full under Luny Tunes. What is his alone is the road that got him there — a childhood spent moving from one house to the next to stay ahead of a dangerous man, and a decade of hits made without his partner once the two of them had already changed what reggaeton sounded like.' WHERE slug = 'luny';

